\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Three operators use the two arrows differently}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{On a \vocabk{recovery site}{fdip-00PQ} of rank #{d}, with both
directions of every axis and with every weight equal to #{1},
the odd pairing of \ref{fdip-00PO} distinguishes the centered
operator, the Wilson operator and the counting operator #{D}.
The centered symbol of \ref{fdip-009R} keeps both shifts of
each axis inside one sine, and its zeros are the #{2^d}
corners. On a finite grid with #{e} even periods,
\ref{fdip-000T} gives that operator kernel dimension
#{2^{d+e}}. The Wilson operator of \ref{fdip-009V}, at trivial
links with #{a>0} and #{r\ne0}, and with every period at least
three, has kernel equal to the constant fields, of dimension
#{2^d q} as stated there. A corner with #{k} coordinates equal
to #{\pi} receives the scalar Wilson term #{2rk/a}. The
counting operator of \ref{fdip-00OL} assigns the forward
difference to #{\epsilon_i} and the backward difference to
#{\iota_i}. Over an ordered field and with positive weights,
\ref{fdip-00OP} gives an isomorphism from the kernel of #{D}
to the cohomology of #{d}, and both have rank #{2^d}. The
fibre is the regular module of \ref{fdip-00OS}, free of that
same rank. For #{N\ge 3} the right multiplications commuting
with #{D} are the scalars, by \ref{fdip-00OT}, so the fibre is
the multiplicity module over that commutant. This lemma calls
the module the taste module, as a name for the left
multiplicity alone. \ref{fdip-009X} asserts no taste
identification for its reducible carrier. On an equal-period
square the counting kernel has rank #{4}, by \ref{fdip-00OU}.}

\proof{
\p{The dimensions are the cited ones. On a Fourier mode the
forward difference contributes the factor #{e^{ip_i}-1} and
the backward difference contributes #{e^{-ip_i}-1}, so one
axis of #{D} acts by
##{(e^{ip_i}-1)\epsilon_i+(e^{-ip_i}-1)\iota_i.}
Twice this operator equals
##{(e^{ip_i}-e^{-ip_i})(\epsilon_i-\iota_i)
+(e^{ip_i}+e^{-ip_i}-2)(\epsilon_i+\iota_i).}
Replacement of #{p_i} by #{-p_i} changes the sign of the first
coefficient and preserves the second. The sine in
\ref{fdip-009R} is a scalar multiple of
#{e^{ip_i}-e^{-ip_i}}, so both shifts remain in that symbol,
and the symbol vanishes at #{0} and at #{\pi}. The scalar
#{2rk/a} of \ref{fdip-009V} is nonzero when #{k\ge 1}. The odd
vector #{v_i=e_{i+}-e_{i-}} of \ref{fdip-00PO} changes sign
when the two arrows are swapped, and the even vector does not.
The counting operator assigns the two arrows to #{\epsilon_i}
and #{\iota_i} separately. Its kernel rank is the rank in
\ref{fdip-00OP}. The corner count in \ref{fdip-000T} is
#{2^{d+e}}.}
}
